            kind: 'concept',
            testing: 'Whether you know why language models make things up, and practical, layered ways to reduce it and catch it.',
            answer: 'A language model is trained to predict likely next words, not to check facts. When it doesn\'t know, the most likely-sounding continuation is still fluent and confident, and that is a hallucination.\n\nIn a product I reduce it in layers. Ground it: retrieve the relevant documents and tell the model to answer only from them. Give it permission to say "I don\'t know" when the sources don\'t contain the answer. Ask for citations, so every claim points to a source a person can check. Use a low temperature for factual tasks. And use structured outputs or tools for things the model is bad at, such as calculations or looking up live data.\n\nThen measure it: a test set with questions whose answers are and aren\'t in the documents, scoring faithfulness, and sampling production answers for review.',
            analogy: 'A student who must answer every exam question, with no marks lost for guessing. When they don\'t know, they write something plausible. You fix it by giving them the textbook, allowing "I don\'t know", and asking them to show which page each answer came from.',
