            id: 'ai-temperature',
            q: 'Explain temperature and top-p. What would you set them to, and why?',
            level: 'beginner',
            kind: 'concept',
            testing: 'Whether you know how the model picks each next token, and can match the settings to the task.',
            answer: 'At each step the model gives a probability for every possible next token, and these settings control how it picks one.\n\nTemperature reshapes those probabilities before sampling. Low temperature sharpens them, so the top token almost always wins: steady, predictable answers. High temperature flattens them, so less likely tokens get picked more often: more variety, but more mistakes.\n\nTop-p, or nucleus sampling, keeps only the smallest set of top tokens whose probabilities add up to p, say 0.9, and samples from those, cutting off the long tail of unlikely tokens.\n\nFor extraction, classification or factual answers I use a low temperature, near 0. For brainstorming or creative writing, higher, around 0.7 to 1. Usually I tune one of the two, not both.',
            analogy: 'Choosing a restaurant. Temperature 0: always go to your favourite. High temperature: sometimes try somewhere odd. Top-p: only ever choose from the places that make up 90% of your usual visits, never from the dodgy ones.',
