            id: 'ai-attention',
            q: 'How does attention in a transformer work, in plain words?',
            level: 'intermediate',
            kind: 'concept',
            testing: 'Whether you understand the core mechanism behind every modern LLM well enough to explain it without the formula.',
            answer: 'Attention lets every token gather information from the other tokens that matter to it.\n\nEach token makes three vectors: a query ("what am I looking for?"), a key ("what do I contain?") and a value ("what will I pass on?"). A token compares its query with every key using a dot product. A high score means "this one is relevant to me". Softmax turns the scores into weights that add up to 1, and the token\'s new representation is the weighted average of all the values.\n\nSo in "The animal didn\'t cross the street because it was tired", the token "it" gives a high weight to "animal", and picks up its meaning.\n\nMulti-head attention runs several of these in parallel, so different heads can track different relationships, like grammar or meaning. In a GPT-style model, a mask stops each token from looking at later tokens.',
            analogy: 'A room of people, each with a question in mind. Everyone holds up a name badge saying what they know (key). You compare your question (query) with every badge, listen most to the best matches, and come away with a blend of what they said (values).',
